+*   **Strengths and Limitations:** GS-IR improves rendering efficiency compared to NeRF-based methods by using forward mapping splatting [S1]. It handles occlusion via baking, making it available for both objects and unbounded scenes, unlike TensoIR which is limited to object-level [S1].
+*   **When to use it and when not:** Use when real-time rendering efficiency is required and the scene is unbounded or object-level. Not suitable when ray-traced occlusion is strictly necessary for high-fidelity indirect lighting without baking approximations.
 
 ## Background
-GS-IR is the first work to introduce the 3DGS technique for inverse rendering, simultaneously estimating scene geometry, materials, and illumination from multi-view images [S1]. It was proposed by researchers from the School of Data Science, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen, and Tencent [S1]. The source code is available at https://github.com/lzhnb/GSIR [S1].
